    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="2" relname="circumstance">Speaking on keyboard design and user-friendliness,</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="30" relname="span">my company recently purchased a few 100 new DELLS for our engineering department.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="28" relname="span">My team's priority was to deploy these new desktops,</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="3" relname="circumstance">while minimizing any user downtime;</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="29" relname="span">much easier said than done.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="5" relname="nonvolitional-cause">The main problem we ran into was the data transferring for each user.</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="31" relname="span">Anyway, by the time we were done with the project</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="25" relname="span">everything seemed ok,</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="8" relname="elaboration">and all the users were much more satisfied with the performance of their new machines.</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="24" relname="span">After a few days my team started getting many complaints that the new keyboards were a problem.</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="33" relname="span">How could they be a problem?</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="37" relname="list">They were brand new,</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="37" relname="list">they matched the new DELLs,</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="37" relname="list">they had multimedia connections,</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="37" relname="list">and they even had 2 USB ports which enabled a user to plug their digital camera or any other USB device into their keyboard without having to use their PC.</segment>
        <segment id="16" parent="38" relname="span">So I was wondering just what the problem was.</segment>
        <segment id="17" parent="34" relname="span">It turned out to be the way the keys were designed on the keyboard.</segment>
        <segment id="18" parent="35" relname="span">The INSERT and DELETE keys were slightly arranged a bit different from the old keyboards.</segment>
        <segment id="19" parent="20" relname="circumstance">When a user depends on the certain key that they use so often</segment>
        <segment id="20" parent="36" relname="span">a small issue like this can create a huge problem.</segment>
        <segment id="21" parent="23" relname="summary">This is a good example of how keyboard design and user involvement can greatly just how satisfied your end-user base will be.</segment>
        <group id="22" type="span"/>
